<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelagonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelagonia is a plain and historical region in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a>, extending into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In antiquity it was the land of the Pelagones, and in the Roman settlement of 167 BCE it formed one of the four republics into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was divided. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Byzantine and Bulgarian borderland and the site of a battle in 1259.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelagonia.jpg" alt="Pelagonia" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelagonija.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pelagonia is a plain shared between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It includes the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a>, in the Pelagonia Statistical Region of North Macedonia, and the city of Florina, in Western Macedonia in Greece. The border crossing at Medžitlija-Niki lies on it. In antiquity it was bounded by Paeonia to the north and east, Lynkestis and Almopia to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> to the west, and corresponded roughly to the present municipalities of Prilep, Bitola, Mogila, Novaci, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a> and Krivogaštani. A tract of 137,000 hectares of the plain is a designated Important Bird Area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region took its name from the Pelagones, an ancient tribe of Upper Macedonia centred on the plain. Greek mythology made their eponym Pelagon a son of the river-god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> and the father of Asteropaeus, a Paeonian leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad. Mycenaean objects have been found in the area and are shown in the Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>. Menelaus of Pelagonia, around 360 BCE, is said to have fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> when Philip II annexed his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Strabo called the region Tripolitis.</p><p>In medieval times Pelagonia took on a broader meaning and was contested for centuries between the Byzantine and Bulgarian empires, before the Serbian Empire annexed it in the 14th century. After that empire fragmented it belonged to the Lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> until the Ottoman conquest at the end of the century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, was fought during the Struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Its site is disputed: the sources name only Boril's Wood, which scholars have placed near Prilep, Kastoria or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a>, and two modern scholars suggest the plain north of Kastoria near Vevi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Florina was taken by the Greek Army from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in November 1912, but the army turned east towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> on news of the Bulgarian advance, and most of the plain was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The Monastir Gap, the open stretch of the Greek border here, was strategically important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, during the Monastir Offensive, and in April 1941, when German forces came through it and took Florina on 10 April before the Battle of Vevi on 11 and 12 April.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelagonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The principality’s decline began with <b>Pelagonia</b> and the cession of the fortresses in 1262, which planted a Byzantine province in the south-east of the peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Drougoubitai settled in the plain west and north-west of Thessalonica, towards the Vardar and <b>Pelagonia</b>, in the early 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/drougoubitai">Drougoubitai</a></li><li><blockquote>In 167 BCE Lucius Aemilius Paullus announced the settlement at Amphipolis: Macedon was to be 'free' but divided into four republics (merides) with capitals at Amphipolis, Thessalonica, Pella and <b>Pelagonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-macedonian-republics">Four Macedonian Republics</a></li></ul></section>
